[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa67265c-58c2-5525-a76e-07d76e4d6c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa67265c-58c2-5525-a76e-07d76e4d6c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319907} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de44fcf-9a78-5bd7-9b48-93e1a12d9e69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6cc98cbb-1926-5fbd-b202-06c3cb8a795d" :question/math-academy-id "q-352867" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=5x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=5x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75781285-f568-5213-b859-431f02aade49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75781285-f568-5213-b859-431f02aade49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319746} :answer-field/id #uuid "c391ebff-e18b-5edc-94f9-bf83e99d6410" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c92829c-a2f6-56f6-ab9b-8cc246269a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-352868" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da1c47f5-5e7e-50ce-b14e-b9f3f3f757ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da1c47f5-5e7e-50ce-b14e-b9f3f3f757ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320054} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4fe866-71a8-5d45-8c02-73304c3706b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50520627-69a0-5672-92d6-61584ad60ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-352870" :question/problem "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-(-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a98b8e62-d040-5b60-bf7e-33fa7ff70166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a98b8e62-d040-5b60-bf7e-33fa7ff70166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c96f03ff-5290-5981-833e-c327c1729242" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9fe919cc-2020-562f-9dce-f16756b25954" :question/math-academy-id "q-352924" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{8}$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{8},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{8x}-2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{8x}+2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{8})|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{4(1/2)}-{e}^{-4(1/2)}}{8}]-[\\frac{{e}^{4(0)}-{e}^{-4(0)}}{8}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{8{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d574d57-80e0-5184-b90e-d3ca614be8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{6{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d574d57-80e0-5184-b90e-d3ca614be8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{6{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319672} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c35a9bf-b9c0-537e-a06c-e7e0dc92951f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13f6ace1-1999-5d34-ac80-deb1a0dcd5d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-352925" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{6x}-2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{6x}+2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3(1)}-{e}^{-3(1)}}{6}]-[\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}-{e}^{-3(0)}}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{6{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29de170a-c6df-528c-9251-01f3de79ef6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319525} {:answer/id #uuid "38e191bb-945f-5f74-84c7-b6dbab73d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319566} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4489fa-8058-58fc-9d16-c24df105f95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319671} {:answer/id #uuid "da2382ba-e1ad-5327-8727-332d59ce6c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320055} {:answer/id #uuid "e11b8b77-8937-57d0-997a-adeec99d9234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38e191bb-945f-5f74-84c7-b6dbab73d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319566} :answer-field/id #uuid "79cea883-b687-5151-a7fb-d722579f29c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2472ea9f-8e8e-5b80-8c7c-945a5a43c650" :question/math-academy-id "q-35297" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab403f1-37e8-5537-b482-1b497f41b6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319614} {:answer/id #uuid "7defa742-522c-5545-9656-199144eab8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319768} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebdd4c7-2b1d-5b85-aec1-f2e0254b78bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319818} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ae9ea2-f7af-5bb3-9254-c194f6f8817e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320039} {:answer/id #uuid "eebdfceb-9ba4-5843-ba91-acb1afc553e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eebdfceb-9ba4-5843-ba91-acb1afc553e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320107} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab218b2-bffd-5950-8dc4-5254fdc0b910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e1095e2-b747-574e-a018-9f92abb5fb55" :question/math-academy-id "q-35299" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{10}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee0dea3-5503-5141-860e-567bd3b36149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cee0dea3-5503-5141-860e-567bd3b36149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320025} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c569726-6bbc-5754-bfd4-86ac748e8587" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a56a5b68-c2a3-5c78-b4ca-8aa4e273c927" :question/math-academy-id "q-353019" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=1+{x}^{2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+{x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+4{x}^{2}.$ Then, $8x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(2)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "267fce2f-8f79-53e9-831e-7c2a90e2adc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319510} {:answer/id #uuid "345eb41a-6222-5988-930d-6af162d31248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319552} {:answer/id #uuid "48471577-d3ba-5647-a23c-9be647a9f57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319607} {:answer/id #uuid "96266788-11c1-5a60-bc6e-f7ab6b25cc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319834} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b8e6ac-3f8b-59ff-9fcc-f59861669f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48471577-d3ba-5647-a23c-9be647a9f57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319607} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca04464-a958-5b61-98f7-1d33e413d898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a41f8fd-4b7f-554c-b544-a6304735d193" :question/math-academy-id "q-35302" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c13576bf-cf5b-5ca4-8b5d-ac2063931cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c13576bf-cf5b-5ca4-8b5d-ac2063931cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319982} :answer-field/id #uuid "15564a3d-2b26-54ad-9b7f-971d85dd9b9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320208} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99622a70-6a54-55f5-8573-86553e0f3e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99622a70-6a54-55f5-8573-86553e0f3e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319851} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a36821d-dc1d-5e88-af5f-ac470b14af37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "412307fe-9131-57d1-97c7-2428bd11fd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412307fe-9131-57d1-97c7-2428bd11fd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319587} :answer-field/id #uuid "928abb11-0bf2-5930-83f0-bc3839c8a4ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb70248c-d191-522a-acca-d1658cbddb31" :question/math-academy-id "q-353173" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{2x}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{2}}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+4}{n+5})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}$ is convergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a4d6edd-79c2-5c74-8dc6-504578e0856a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a4d6edd-79c2-5c74-8dc6-504578e0856a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319855} :answer-field/id #uuid "8912c0bd-736a-5e2d-a592-ca76e1eaee47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ffc0670-7032-5618-9938-5d5033bcb5b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-353210" :question/problem "The number of bacteria $N=N(t)$ in a laboratory culture $t$ hours after observation can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $500$ bacteria initially, and $N(3)=900$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$%$" :question/worked-solution "We model the number of bacteria using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $500$ bacteria initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of bacteria $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=900$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 900 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{900}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ k & ≈0.1959\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈20%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5308342e-0079-53b8-96c0-a1226d25ca27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5308342e-0079-53b8-96c0-a1226d25ca27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319631} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fff7544-68af-54e4-a42e-daa502e7690e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f112590-ba59-50ed-81c5-89311d726307" :question/math-academy-id "q-353211" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $S=S(t)$ to a website $t$ months after a marketing campaign can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, and $S(2)=15\\,000$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$%$" :question/worked-solution "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $S(2)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(2) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 2} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{2k} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ 2k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & ≈0.111\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈11%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105f64a7-64e1-5add-aae7-770c5b157588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105f64a7-64e1-5add-aae7-770c5b157588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319436} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec78df54-217a-5610-9cb8-7a27f0cb9faa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a9a6fe7-11ca-57f2-9394-dc75bd8e2e74" :question/math-academy-id "q-353217" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /8} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5ad25e-d9b1-5c85-8097-39a50138dc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d5ad25e-d9b1-5c85-8097-39a50138dc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319476} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ee6d87-9e1a-5ca4-a7e2-2bd66c9eb811" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "286990a3-3f35-503f-8eaf-0cc7f8896a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-353218" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+{sec}^{2}⁡(\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "750df33d-b115-587e-9ed1-ce73862fa86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "750df33d-b115-587e-9ed1-ce73862fa86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319745} :answer-field/id #uuid "24f26ab2-97f3-5fbf-99ad-5430dea8249c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffacd058-e0e5-543f-8b9e-1afffd584455" :question/math-academy-id "q-353226" :question/problem "![](images/06/064e75694d82bed2177df55e2e66c3343a2c0396cd54f5d4304f952a01abb219.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\theta $ (shown above) evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$   at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9ecbbab-e091-5ef8-8f70-9fa410474a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9ecbbab-e091-5ef8-8f70-9fa410474a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320009} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83baf89-97b8-55e1-b59c-b52e1b0ecb11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb4d30fd-268a-5c12-85e7-7c896536b59c" :question/math-academy-id "q-353232" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1fe71fe51ae93fc2e78b52b1d3f1c270ab05d10c317345bf1440b13c570483f.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=30$ (shown above), evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=30,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc9260ba-5ec7-53bc-abe4-0ac9cecd25ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9260ba-5ec7-53bc-abe4-0ac9cecd25ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319964} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7d9e6a-4bec-5d25-817f-d42dd5fa88dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6357fd-9ea7-5406-b8cc-4035a2ad90d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-353287" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5406c158-faef-5fc2-afb0-23ad128fff7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5406c158-faef-5fc2-afb0-23ad128fff7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319635} :answer-field/id #uuid "627ea144-5e9c-5334-98b6-906dcf4ad27f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e74cc3fc-655c-51c1-a449-0108494451f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-353288" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{y},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{2}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1580189-cceb-5df8-8538-e6f6e2e557a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1580189-cceb-5df8-8538-e6f6e2e557a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320034} :answer-field/id #uuid "2afec4ee-30fd-563d-b5f8-243641b7f38b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b63d46f-2093-5c38-a5e6-8e0cc48a1edb" :question/math-academy-id "q-353306" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x+1},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Fill in the empty box in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{2y}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)}{0+1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{2})≈4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d907775-3c53-589e-b042-2d5eb1fb60ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d907775-3c53-589e-b042-2d5eb1fb60ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319723} :answer-field/id #uuid "975bfb7f-04ba-54d4-b66a-54a1e4d777e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "106429bb-a000-54b0-bc60-874e5ed8aa6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-353325" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2y-1),\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0f10ff0-18c6-5914-a313-73bb4cb93ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0f10ff0-18c6-5914-a313-73bb4cb93ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319929} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c2d3244-6f8c-503e-84d8-0399c320617e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d193d39c-471b-5fec-8b86-474bbc1fb6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-353341" :question/problem "![](images/18/1817c65aea37112d93f2935801a0e7dcd654706a72f729810272f48e95d39665.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /6}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab36b16-5bde-52a2-af10-016c5c78e4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab36b16-5bde-52a2-af10-016c5c78e4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3297beab-a596-5653-8a3c-f59721811ee1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1a4b6d5-bbe2-5c0f-8f42-413948326feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-353346" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=8t-3,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=4.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}=16\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =16\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =16(2) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644} :answer-field/id #uuid "88549d85-6690-5559-9bdd-e3b23c490bbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88d2c31a-1162-5af9-962c-a704ed1fd8ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-353350" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abab926e465522d30b236577bf8a1654502807b864c9fed8a4cebb84efffd3a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568} :answer-field/id #uuid "6920c4ef-fcdf-5331-9ff1-0d0ddf66b7c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6474a49e-c58a-508a-9e50-a787079f364a" :question/math-academy-id "q-353351" :question/problem "![](images/35/35c62ccf48c4d0a49729da04b443cb94d112e673e0c24e7784d22f48eaabf8f7.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "982a8458-75dc-5100-af04-e624afa1e452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "982a8458-75dc-5100-af04-e624afa1e452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319841} :answer-field/id #uuid "a53b9fcf-ef2f-5a14-bac4-17146292621b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbfa04e6-3190-5004-ae3c-42d7b0e370d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-353403" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{3x+1}$ that lies between the lines $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3x+1})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{12x+13}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{(12x+13)^{3/2}}{18}\\,{|}_{-1/3}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}({25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{49\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0070d0c-5b62-5010-8c4b-27af45dbdbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0070d0c-5b62-5010-8c4b-27af45dbdbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319926} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcedab8c-1deb-556f-9926-cc02cb1148f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f32af2d2-7cae-54f6-8ab7-dcc5158284d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-353405" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2x+1$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)=2\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}((1+1)-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fa608d2-ea23-579b-99b5-86dc8bf76bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{149\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fa608d2-ea23-579b-99b5-86dc8bf76bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{149\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319728} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf3347d-c3d2-5d29-b421-50af644d511e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45fafd7e-c4e0-5aad-bb72-d9441bb38d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-353411" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2})=2\\sqrt{y}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=1+4y\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}y,\\,y=\\frac{u-1}{4},\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{u-1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {9}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {9}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 243-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 27)-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{396}{5}+\\frac{4}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{149\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c17047-668a-58eb-8811-2926326d851c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319502} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4a3f1f-7d08-5227-be4a-e896b95c3eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{87}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319538} {:answer/id #uuid "6253e536-9aec-5ff7-a639-01423f0aba1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{35}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319689} {:answer/id #uuid "813b0ce5-6ef1-54f0-b04c-a23eb4675381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319781} {:answer/id #uuid "e085f304-6717-501f-b1a1-8799a8d0dcc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6253e536-9aec-5ff7-a639-01423f0aba1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{35}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319689} :answer-field/id #uuid "b756c1f9-a0b1-5c42-be8c-b2d7d2464ccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8d868ef-2de9-5254-a92c-6c21a4194c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-353424" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x(y-2),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with one step to approximate $y(1).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table and add the initial condition data.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x(y-2) \\\\  & =3(0)(1-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =3{x}_{1}({y}_{1}-2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =3{x}_{2}({y}_{2}-2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{21}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+(-\\frac{21}{8})\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,-\\frac{13}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =3{x}_{3}({y}_{3}-2) \\\\  & =3(1)(-\\frac{13}{8}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{87}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{21}{8}$ | $-\\frac{87}{8}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{3}{2})+2\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{21}{8})+(-\\frac{87}{8}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\,-\\frac{24}{8}-\\frac{42}{8}-\\frac{87}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{153}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{21}{8}$ | $-\\frac{87}{8}$ | $-\\frac{51}{16}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+(-\\frac{51}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{35}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{21}{8}$ | $-\\frac{87}{8}$ | $-\\frac{51}{16}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{35}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-\\frac{35}{16}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b62aea-e3f4-523f-a902-d1a9e4779b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319406} {:answer/id #uuid "7735440e-e7f0-5b70-a465-136a465a1efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319749} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d17f6e-8b29-519d-8ae5-8066c3d20ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319892} {:answer/id #uuid "c189cbe0-0365-5aef-b868-68145c4dfacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319985} {:answer/id #uuid "ecac3b29-1726-5db0-9775-040e9c14b3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04b62aea-e3f4-523f-a902-d1a9e4779b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319406} :answer-field/id #uuid "abd222bd-6da0-5c4b-b26f-2a42968d72ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a52db54f-ed58-568b-ad92-d2a1074b04ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-35348" :question/problem "Given $Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(Z))?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(Z))$, we reduce $Z$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{5} & :={R}_{5}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $4$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(Z))=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d19fd66-2731-5e3e-9dd2-9505457e34e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d19fd66-2731-5e3e-9dd2-9505457e34e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319669} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b975067-7f73-5968-a997-f1dc926431ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d92e703c-8485-513b-8983-fd7fd22219c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-353520" :question/problem "The half-life of cobalt-60 is $5.27$ years. How long does it take for a $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of cobalt-60 to reduce to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{yr}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5.27$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5.27=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{8.0} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{16}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $4$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=4\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=4\\cdot 5.27=21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98827adc-2ae4-5e58-89ee-13340a8d90f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98827adc-2ae4-5e58-89ee-13340a8d90f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319842} :answer-field/id #uuid "31423f8a-cf58-5641-ab59-0f4d5ca498ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c2b0789-a049-58b0-a4c0-cb60d52adde7" :question/math-academy-id "q-353523" :question/problem "The half-life of fluorine-18 is $109.7$ minutes. How long does it take for a $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ sample of fluorine-18 to reduce to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{min}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=109.7$ minutes, we have\n\n$$\n109.7=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=6.4\\,\\text{ng},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{6.4} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{32} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{32}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡32}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =548.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.2\\,\\text{ng}}{6.4\\,\\text{ng}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $5$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=5\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=5\\cdot 109.7=548.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1b56a7-f6ec-58b3-a41e-b0232d847e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "415.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f1b56a7-f6ec-58b3-a41e-b0232d847e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "415.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319537} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba55605-f61b-5f9b-8ee0-6f8d137abe0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d67921e3-14ad-5197-b390-1f8f4c638645" :question/math-academy-id "q-353546" :question/problem "The half-life of polonium-210 is $138.4$ days. How long does it take for a $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of polonium-210 to reduce to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{days}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=138.4$ days, we have\n\n$$\n138.4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1.1}{8.8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =415.2\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 138.4=415.2\\,\\text{days}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd1ec179-c3eb-579d-b407-4fb26d5fa6e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320154} {:answer/id #uuid "048e136b-84ec-5868-b091-1a12da7bcf24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(e^{\\pi}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "048e136b-84ec-5868-b091-1a12da7bcf24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(e^{\\pi}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498739} :answer-field/id #uuid "2092fbc0-9454-5cb0-afd6-99345b4a8c65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e21eed1a-d586-5617-ae1f-53bbb8801576" :question/math-academy-id "q-353598" :question/problem "![](images/16/16b676776dc22aece5a1fa777007a03b9e7903706cbdf5cc4ae14d8508370419.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{[{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2(\\pi /2)}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ec07961-28ad-57ab-9dc2-935afbeeb1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec07961-28ad-57ab-9dc2-935afbeeb1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319580} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb6204f-3eb4-5f37-91f5-6c3112218559" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c077a15-44c0-5080-9f91-c561226617cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-353605" :question/problem "Find the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r={3}^{\\theta /4}$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}$\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡3+16}$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta /4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta /4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta /4})}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta /4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{{3}^{\\theta /2}+{3}^{\\theta /2}\\cdot \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}){3}^{\\theta /2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{3}^{\\theta /4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta /4}\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{1}-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡3+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b47beba-991b-5909-a5c9-c186e8fd9482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b47beba-991b-5909-a5c9-c186e8fd9482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321604} :answer-field/id #uuid "037543f6-a414-55b1-ab84-706b79a0fd89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c758642-6704-5c98-a369-54f599e78abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c758642-6704-5c98-a369-54f599e78abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321747} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd890e3-39f7-5304-b611-2d5698616773" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323289} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d83b61-92fa-531a-81dd-8377ef8229e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d83b61-92fa-531a-81dd-8377ef8229e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322170} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c15c25-31ea-5ebe-93d8-33b1a903d95d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17018abc-8b11-54e7-a5d9-de971a73a6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-353904" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf661fd-f365-5b43-b6db-0d733698432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf661fd-f365-5b43-b6db-0d733698432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321874} :answer-field/id #uuid "278ab5db-7128-588b-a004-990c8486b2af" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04955447-b0cf-51ce-87be-b29665ad544b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04955447-b0cf-51ce-87be-b29665ad544b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321191} :answer-field/id #uuid "59f9a8a5-d007-57b6-a0ce-a1ac57e4383d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d22d59-eb87-5008-9ff4-0e180d544e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3d22d59-eb87-5008-9ff4-0e180d544e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322437} :answer-field/id #uuid "6213c0e3-56bb-5f52-ab5c-43a2de919e58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ea0e4bd-2ba0-5ea8-a5c9-cf5026dd4498" :question/math-academy-id "q-353917" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ centered about the point $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}\\,+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1. \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1+0\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61093b51-5d5c-52ad-8842-70788b1e7aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61093b51-5d5c-52ad-8842-70788b1e7aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321908} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9d53f60-8bd1-506f-882a-dc098eb9f15b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ea0e9ed-64e4-52f1-b7df-82d492346b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-354043" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-{csc}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-1+(-2)(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-2(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2$ into the above to approximate $cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2) & ≈-1-2(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-1-2(0.2) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94b21ad1-1bcb-538e-9d14-8438c0ee8ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b21ad1-1bcb-538e-9d14-8438c0ee8ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322329} :answer-field/id #uuid "9840e670-77c7-5f8d-b791-c8ed628ab0e8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323462} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68ceb31f-a541-5a29-8ba5-d72d76e45f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68ceb31f-a541-5a29-8ba5-d72d76e45f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321965} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc935f36-6a74-584a-9b42-9568edc6138b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b43bcef-d3e4-53de-9882-a31e4b89dee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-354997" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ 4t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-48\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-48<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$  Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =3 \\\\ 0+C & =3\\,⟹\\,C=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f9f38f1-6cd1-5c7b-ac85-370a6ef2dfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f9f38f1-6cd1-5c7b-ac85-370a6ef2dfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321647} :answer-field/id #uuid "934c4ee2-16ce-5843-8f2f-dfd84dc4a965" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcd16528-ff7b-51a9-b750-56eb3afb17be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcd16528-ff7b-51a9-b750-56eb3afb17be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322875} :answer-field/id #uuid "b76df0d7-0736-5b94-b5c9-1432140c8861" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fdcb4a35-fcad-5ff2-b2a2-88ba2f60d1cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-354998" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ 5t & =\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{2\\pi }{5},\\frac{3\\pi }{5},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C & =2 \\\\ -2+C & =2 \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02709403-b870-584e-98ba-6c863f227d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321170} {:answer/id #uuid "0b11217e-c071-5ec6-ac11-eab13c676a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321237} {:answer/id #uuid "23b68c39-1ad8-5ba8-8266-734fae77a185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321420} {:answer/id #uuid "75a44b6e-9f06-5fb9-95f2-f4dc45a9d9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322064} {:answer/id #uuid "ebec7e42-ccf2-59d1-a11a-4093a463037c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02709403-b870-584e-98ba-6c863f227d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321170} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c47907-1c5c-5193-9fe8-a1540f384554" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f50f445-3873-5d48-8eec-e37880dda36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35513" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470} :answer-field/id #uuid "549d1fa9-dbbb-5a28-9ec0-95d21eef029c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a3b691d-ce23-5509-8fc2-d490e3df8940" :question/math-academy-id "q-355525" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y){y}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=0$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c5bd0e-992a-5060-a1b1-7adb29cef3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c5bd0e-992a-5060-a1b1-7adb29cef3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f6b0da-0d33-5c51-ac9b-a4198b00fb73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "805542c0-bca8-5f60-bf27-701c39eb6996" :question/math-academy-id "q-355576" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{0}+C) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ C-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2{e}^{2}+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a653501-7f39-554f-8700-17ab8e35a4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a653501-7f39-554f-8700-17ab8e35a4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322252} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c651f7-74fe-5f62-ad22-da3d8a63750e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44820654-043f-529e-8512-cf2fa8eaf9b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-356029" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-3$ and $y=1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/66/664fcc791ec42b4ec40ed3fc7033fa601d646c9900632864122d31fd01a2cf61.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/1d/1d10b1994774239767578417bc5f0b74a6a7369005a73fbac859574c9d5d36e0.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(1)-({x}^{2}-3)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-(-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500} :answer-field/id #uuid "2032fb96-09c8-5ded-b5bd-deeda3577ddf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b201f2-c9f2-5028-a916-abafa9097eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587316a5-f847-5f83-912c-b52ebafdaeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-357255" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02365f64-5f8c-566c-a522-523a978e5e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321168} {:answer/id #uuid "8015e52d-c714-5fad-9fc0-fc9ff19ddbbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322152} {:answer/id #uuid "8c323b73-baef-507e-917b-e46dc735a5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322268} {:answer/id #uuid "d673765d-648f-52bf-8827-db6da877b49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322821} {:answer/id #uuid "fb040589-7b1a-5f83-9142-68e2dc95668b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb040589-7b1a-5f83-9142-68e2dc95668b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323126} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44c3910-a676-5e6f-b07c-e38c6a923181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d22c3d57-064f-5a29-85f5-aa8e18bd8665" :question/math-academy-id "q-35830" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{9{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+11}{12{x}^{5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+11}{12{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{12{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+0+0}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16123c5b-0e24-5448-94af-edee5d7a2379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321327} {:answer/id #uuid "54a80482-3b1f-51c4-b743-a176cf719e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321813} {:answer/id #uuid "570b242a-d6a5-5843-b3d2-25a379d35ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321834} {:answer/id #uuid "6575a5ba-b2fa-552c-a16a-377b10470e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321933} {:answer/id #uuid "af8740d1-b898-5366-95a3-967eda7c4f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "570b242a-d6a5-5843-b3d2-25a379d35ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321834} :answer-field/id #uuid "0795df74-4d2f-57e9-b4f9-ecdee6b9ed2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a34c28ce-0cb3-5e56-ab36-3c5183b046d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35831" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a83d4ad1-4592-577a-9a6b-f5fa68bf90b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83d4ad1-4592-577a-9a6b-f5fa68bf90b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322464} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f29d86e-519b-56da-bf1c-57233f56e8b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "615eeedf-3ba4-59d8-9d3a-634f4529d73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-358782" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,12t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(1)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{12}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{12}{13})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{5}{13})}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5871484e-21bb-5092-b09c-e08bf26d1e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321842} {:answer/id #uuid "a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442589} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9dd7521-e5a8-502d-93ab-04dd55f565ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77a13f7-0f57-5d23-af8b-ebbc48aa1422" :question/math-academy-id "q-358783" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c0c3933-f2eb-5430-9130-c222c7dd3385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321486} {:answer/id #uuid "2dced506-c98b-54f2-b521-a33b0d31abca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321505} {:answer/id #uuid "80578236-5da7-59f5-bd63-88412bcc2467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{cot}^{-1}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322155} {:answer/id #uuid "b8492909-14e1-53f7-8fa9-31eb444b5dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322580} {:answer/id #uuid "f8841ffb-1d88-5c6c-a30f-dff18090dbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cot}^{-1}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dced506-c98b-54f2-b521-a33b0d31abca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321505} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8e3c92-c6bd-5872-9009-f104c52acf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ddbb116-5940-52b1-bf90-b7efeb6e8633" :question/math-academy-id "q-3588" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+20 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+20 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+20 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-4}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb97f34-32f2-5fbb-aec5-57ace0fbaad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321872} {:answer/id #uuid "8b638a94-901f-5e77-bbce-f68bb55836a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322260} {:answer/id #uuid "c656713f-8a67-5fd6-8734-5401564b9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322683} {:answer/id #uuid "d689d4b8-8b89-5de7-b04a-9ad318e85b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322822} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7edee3-ab4b-5148-91d5-eeb3f01579ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c656713f-8a67-5fd6-8734-5401564b9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322683} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9b73dc-645a-581e-bb60-7a672cb6e2c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84868fd8-3ce0-5bf2-96b8-b86160226a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-3594" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-8x-15 & =-({x}^{2}+8x+15) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+[{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}]+15) \\\\  & =-((x+4)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-8x-15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9fc2f6-55bd-579f-8d29-1c23432bc9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321268} {:answer/id #uuid "60e28288-b23d-5dcd-b0df-f485ee49c892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321907} {:answer/id #uuid "67ff6316-9900-5d46-835b-dc3f73fa9490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321959} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7eefa4-73e4-5852-8337-87a7151461a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322262} {:answer/id #uuid "e6558f18-e9d0-5aff-84ab-a80892c690b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f9fc2f6-55bd-579f-8d29-1c23432bc9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321268} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c408a79-7d5b-5c64-bf80-a6ec1301f01a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dc17c5e-dc05-517c-b33a-803dd069587a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36009" :question/problem "Evaluate the gradient of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ if $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟨x,\\,y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\,0) & =\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\,\\frac{1}{4}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1767a3-1f5e-5513-8f07-b57db720be84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|-\\frac{3}{x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321265} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbb9d43-569b-505a-964a-5abffc82feed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321618} {:answer/id #uuid "87335f01-34a2-5e0e-84bd-153053b9b954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}|-\\frac{1}{x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322220} {:answer/id #uuid "87850f46-b969-54a6-9efc-aa99c23f6b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}|+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5{x}^{2}|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322224} {:answer/id #uuid "9e54ad9a-e9f2-5760-9f4c-cf94a74c6cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e54ad9a-e9f2-5760-9f4c-cf94a74c6cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322394} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f868f3-e53c-5bf4-ae3e-d2c4e263b9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c90ef52c-ba49-546e-a3d2-a0b1595aa340" :question/math-academy-id "q-3609" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by ${x}^{2}(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x-5=A(x)(x-5)+B(x-5)+C{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({5}^{2})-2(5)-5 & =A(5)(5-5)+B(5-5)+C(5)^{2} \\\\ 10 & =C(25) \\\\ C & =\\frac{10}{25} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-2(0)-5 & =A(0)(0-5)+B(0-5)+C(0)^{2} \\\\ -5 & =B(-5) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-5}{-5} \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({1}^{2})-2(1)-5 & =A(1)(1-5)+B(1-5)+C(1)^{2} \\\\ -6 & =-4A-4B+C \\\\ -6 & =-4A-4(1)+(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 4A & =6-4+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 4A & =\\frac{10+2}{5} \\\\ A & =\\frac{12}{20} \\\\ A & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}=\\frac{3}{5x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{2}(x-5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{5x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{5(x-5)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a07e8c-0d67-55a0-881c-3c7ad3584a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321306} {:answer/id #uuid "143c3dc4-b275-59ab-82da-1687d8c3670b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321312} {:answer/id #uuid "408f6414-20da-5d9d-9573-95a8e2965353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321657} {:answer/id #uuid "791821d2-5503-575c-8113-5c6cf088de0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322100} {:answer/id #uuid "8cad3b73-177a-52f5-b52e-90d072312464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143c3dc4-b275-59ab-82da-1687d8c3670b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321312} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb63bad1-66a8-55a7-a0fa-643ca9560f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d5da43-5132-5ee1-8491-d36fb395f680" :question/math-academy-id "q-3615" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}+\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-3 & =(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get  $A=\\frac{3}{2},$ $B=-\\frac{3}{2},$ and $C=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{3x-3}{2({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3x-3}{2({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-2x+3.$ Then $(2x-2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-2x+3| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af545d0-dcb5-5648-ab4f-0b94b13d63e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{1}{2x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321864} {:answer/id #uuid "93d40251-d875-5f5d-aef0-72ef06d9ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{2}{x-1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322322} {:answer/id #uuid "d60bd1aa-c2d7-5998-9acb-c89cddd5adb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+\\frac{2}{x-1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322817} {:answer/id #uuid "e6778e04-6c08-54d2-a3df-10a120183457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322951} {:answer/id #uuid "e726d17c-3f3d-5cec-800c-b9ec3bd91662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e726d17c-3f3d-5cec-800c-b9ec3bd91662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322958} :answer-field/id #uuid "883e337e-d198-5733-933e-966f03198bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "acee9da4-09d4-567b-82f2-79a4b4042b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3617" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD={4}^{2}-4(1)(-1)=20\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{20}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+9x-4=A({x}^{2}+4x-1)+(Bx+C)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=1$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4x-1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-1|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae13bb9-55f3-5345-93c6-f0902c6bda50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae13bb9-55f3-5345-93c6-f0902c6bda50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322860} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8150fb-fd9e-5f6f-b6d1-de7e62140686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e7ec77f-c052-5912-b0a1-099aaa7af7c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-361705" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3{sin}^{2}⁡x+7{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{sin}^{2}⁡x+7{cos}^{2}⁡x & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x+3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+4{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3+4{cos}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3+4{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[3+\\frac{4}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})]-[5\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+1]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4b0279-8e87-5626-99b1-6e308ce668c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b4b0279-8e87-5626-99b1-6e308ce668c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322116} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d5ac7c-9c1d-52e6-a055-74d75c1afb2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "292b6739-634d-583f-b073-f74b62cabddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-361711" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8aa97ff1ff27ec11df6aefd4523ed9d39efac686c777f7242701aa2f4fef79c.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, as shown above. The area of the region is\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)+2-x]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+3-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}] \\\\  & =[\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}]-[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a620de-8bd0-57cc-9ca8-627d570b10b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321285} {:answer/id #uuid "539a1518-741f-5932-ab88-ffb0b81d4bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321805} {:answer/id #uuid "6d69e5bf-08b0-519c-bd8e-5f0d93d50b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321997} {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322497} {:answer/id #uuid "c761e53e-5917-50ee-b4b8-03842b140243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322497} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a86fdda-9d41-5703-bec7-0aa41e4dbc4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c3c463e-b1e6-5da9-a9e8-9ab02a63ca2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36227" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa96cf1-4d31-5deb-b3f4-3a3294848a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa96cf1-4d31-5deb-b3f4-3a3294848a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321270} :answer-field/id #uuid "17fe46a9-616b-5b04-b80d-53e10861f0fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39b5bd8a-dd36-5527-89de-e36f567f0906" :question/math-academy-id "q-362509" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a639e1-ea30-5852-945d-838c01f3a530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322245} {:answer/id #uuid "80e254bb-01fa-560a-b35d-95dbd95c9a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e254bb-01fa-560a-b35d-95dbd95c9a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436409} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbc300f-8435-5b63-80a7-df2fbc495791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a3e4c61-3446-503d-9221-974875074079" :question/math-academy-id "q-362530" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb021270-a587-52a3-90c1-6e371b9245f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb021270-a587-52a3-90c1-6e371b9245f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322725} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f0eab9-3abe-5ec9-987c-6dcdda6496ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e12233f-3a34-57d8-bf38-b36992ad1033" :question/math-academy-id "q-362546" :question/problem "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(2-{x}^{2})^{-1/2}(-2x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}))|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})]-[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}})] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe904164-bd58-57b3-8afe-99b03b578a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe904164-bd58-57b3-8afe-99b03b578a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323154} :answer-field/id #uuid "b444c433-7aa2-55ff-a3aa-c36421992c5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22f34c0a-d2be-545d-8a94-5b6c02bfb19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-362589" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ $a=-1,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1-2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1-x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+x}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+1}-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6a3d9d4-e052-50e7-8d60-ebf1d18b86e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6a3d9d4-e052-50e7-8d60-ebf1d18b86e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322952} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4c1c9b8-4757-52e8-84b9-64d59325b020" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ab5bb68-6c78-5933-86bc-f3182668702a" :question/math-academy-id "q-362590" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1+2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2+2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1+x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-1}-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-0}) \\\\  & =0-(-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7048b4f-eab9-53d1-89fb-1d65b5316873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{4}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7048b4f-eab9-53d1-89fb-1d65b5316873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{4}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322455} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb3e809-f5f1-5a7d-8ffc-3c402b2e5aaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd07d931-c8c4-5e1c-9858-8aed94aa2f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-362689" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{4}^{0}{e}^{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{4}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{4}^{0} \\\\  & =-3({e}^{0}-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =-3(1-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{4}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7841411-b2a3-5f14-a4fa-569475a21ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7841411-b2a3-5f14-a4fa-569475a21ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323091} :answer-field/id #uuid "274f1f5a-362f-555e-b4c6-9b261c2ad7ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "053d1129-5ad9-5392-8059-7eab09c97e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-362804" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{csc}^{4}⁡x-1}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{csc}^{4}⁡x-1}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{({csc}^{2}⁡x-1)({csc}^{2}⁡x+1)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}({csc}^{2}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b38ac94-ddcc-5d36-9013-9df4a3ad1196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b38ac94-ddcc-5d36-9013-9df4a3ad1196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322115} :answer-field/id #uuid "604de9af-7501-5d6d-8f16-7e19f3aabd4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81c9f782-ecf0-5e7b-a8aa-08e741da57f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-363485" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={2}^{x}$ about $x=2$ to approximate the value of ${2}^{2.1}.$ Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n${2}^{2.1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+\\frac{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}(x-2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2.1.$ Since $2.1-2=0.1=\\frac{1}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2.1} & ≈4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(\\frac{1}{10})+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{2}^{2.1}≈4.29.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3add13d4-a814-5f0b-aa78-b8fbe0e663e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3add13d4-a814-5f0b-aa78-b8fbe0e663e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321599} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b10121-95e3-50b8-87ee-7d7fd482db00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d857d55e-0426-522a-bbf3-e599533bce03" :question/math-academy-id "q-363486" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={5}^{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of ${5}^{1.3}.$ Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\n${5}^{1.3}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={5}^{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={5}^{x} \\\\ f(1) & =5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={5}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.3.$ Since $1.3-1=0.3=\\frac{3}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{1.3} & ≈5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈8.0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{5}^{1.3}≈8.0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352} :answer-field/id #uuid "abecd077-f709-5b82-9c40-6c4dc262a11d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae92af54-aa04-5ea8-bfcd-575703012570" :question/math-academy-id "q-363490" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6dcfdc0-0425-58da-9863-8d71fb334ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6dcfdc0-0425-58da-9863-8d71fb334ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322824} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebc2e95-bd33-54eb-b38e-19227069b90a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5749738c-9ac0-51f9-9b71-e3d7fdfbde49" :question/math-academy-id "q-364639" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5527a2df-a2d6-5eff-8e35-66b2ae6e3318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5527a2df-a2d6-5eff-8e35-66b2ae6e3318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321819} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdcaa383-804e-5471-975f-ae53c9f7d704" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9ff4e40-981d-5344-8025-a467141eea82" :question/math-academy-id "q-364640" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115a27e6-69f7-5ae7-ae89-7370d3de0757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321283} {:answer/id #uuid "1919937a-2afb-50ef-a435-2e2586b2ccf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321345} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc3e1db-f5f9-598a-b7f8-db2c3fd3fd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321385} {:answer/id #uuid "23de5c13-9552-53a1-b324-42e2d6bbb04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321423} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4fe2b-59cd-55ee-9d03-6090d8c7e680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1919937a-2afb-50ef-a435-2e2586b2ccf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321345} :answer-field/id #uuid "abbe930b-f8f7-558e-aba8-3023dce76a4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08e9021a-643b-5729-9c28-b1707d9de34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-365299" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}\\le 3\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 3\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x} & \\le 9\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\sqrt{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8b3b6f-beec-552d-9868-ed6a0f8961df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321259} {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} {:answer/id #uuid "5f13a860-aaa5-50a7-bd99-bf37fedc3d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321896} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb5e32-2058-558c-8345-f421ebabdca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322111} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89b622-3090-56f6-b614-bf5d1b191223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec706b48-8cdc-560f-b7d1-8876a9a34e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4215e98b-bff5-5507-9fa2-a2d9f87a2aa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-365326" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}>{3}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2} & >{3}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡[3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb9a735-2591-53ae-942e-27326f05c5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321365} {:answer/id #uuid "855765a6-c18c-5fd7-aff3-ae9f740b5f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322201} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4a67bf-2910-5ff0-8c64-ff503d74b674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322377} {:answer/id #uuid "9f100973-b174-52ef-a6df-75a6b6c21f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "855765a6-c18c-5fd7-aff3-ae9f740b5f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322201} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a21df8-6735-53f7-ab0d-60e13e0d8a56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca56064-d1a3-5c0b-9fb2-4735a5356b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(2-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321492} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6fc383-06f8-50a9-8448-ef484256d26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322133} {:answer/id #uuid "d300781a-7ab5-51eb-9dc7-ddbdf2036e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322797} {:answer/id #uuid "e6cd5d3b-afe9-59e8-9ebe-5df84d2f261d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6fc383-06f8-50a9-8448-ef484256d26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322133} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e7b7a9-a761-5a17-a1f6-84fc6f78eb63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df1e3a35-d035-58fe-be59-0f6465258bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-365332" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+1 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb6f943-3e8e-524b-bbee-63f9e2fdf804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321754} {:answer/id #uuid "54d366a4-512b-5fdd-9452-97813292a5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321814} {:answer/id #uuid "6f858266-867e-57c2-9517-65c1befc7cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322012} {:answer/id #uuid "cd53bee2-25b5-5e3c-aed5-988b8fa7045b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd53bee2-25b5-5e3c-aed5-988b8fa7045b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322745} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ca1972-a54a-5852-a21a-a87c720ccc46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4c9f40-c6e2-5853-8638-356f8cad7b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321258} {:answer/id #uuid "32813799-ef84-58e9-be1c-355927fc7c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t(3-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321532} {:answer/id #uuid "4c24bdc6-7b19-5dd6-a83b-07278fa751a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321745} {:answer/id #uuid "cbefe572-e72b-538f-9b17-156c67a987e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c24bdc6-7b19-5dd6-a83b-07278fa751a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321745} :answer-field/id #uuid "efeea11b-c191-58de-8be1-d05cf1c4bccf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00d0a81e-18e6-583c-927a-87161040247c" :question/math-academy-id "q-365372" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+3)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+3 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6ef500-69ff-5a40-b48f-e088f52d064e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321609} {:answer/id #uuid "41c0fa4d-a126-55ae-9c76-9a51eaa582da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321668} {:answer/id #uuid "62b0acb1-9d3b-5014-b1bf-5710aa542d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321916} {:answer/id #uuid "b45af41b-34de-5ff1-9276-8aba652c1ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b45af41b-34de-5ff1-9276-8aba652c1ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322556} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f2eb47-f60c-52cf-b160-334d25e2b44c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e30663a-b390-5830-9a84-dc8051984585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321891} {:answer/id #uuid "863d1538-1168-517a-85c4-b6d2f6ebd3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322213} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6efe1b-c862-552d-9ced-a139644857f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322405} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7b0d79-f075-5a45-a49c-489071c257d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "863d1538-1168-517a-85c4-b6d2f6ebd3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322213} :answer-field/id #uuid "97e590c8-328b-57ee-86d8-da7fa00acd41" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c23b4e5b-85ca-5730-b4e3-0f8ebb92441d" :question/math-academy-id "q-365373" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt-1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt-1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt-1 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ce3693-01de-5937-98a5-950f5433a6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321540} {:answer/id #uuid "65e5517e-cf2c-5731-a1c0-3c543be6b09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321936} {:answer/id #uuid "9991e042-4e75-5222-93c4-5563e233ad9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322367} {:answer/id #uuid "fe04bc55-e4b6-5550-9da1-eee961886cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9991e042-4e75-5222-93c4-5563e233ad9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322367} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d07bac-0abb-5ec1-be1f-93cfaff8da9c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323168} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0392f3cb-4495-5d45-8af7-ce324dce5c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321180} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecc4064-5657-5ce5-b134-f78f11f3c2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322281} {:answer/id #uuid "b980b6a7-aeff-58c4-9680-5fe1df4c9844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322586} {:answer/id #uuid "be505855-bad5-5c93-997b-278b7d7030eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be505855-bad5-5c93-997b-278b7d7030eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322625} :answer-field/id #uuid "8142cc63-340f-5ac3-8562-6ecae5fa9382" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4790775-7e4a-5504-8e0d-e0c3f96089b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-365376" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=xsin\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=xsin\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\ (t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1 \\\\ (t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t} & =\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083ce095-68ad-5438-94f0-d1ad6f101d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321211} {:answer/id #uuid "34fe18e9-f3bb-5fa3-acad-95f48e7d367d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321554} {:answer/id #uuid "409b6d70-1792-5fef-b190-3dd3abe8fb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321659} {:answer/id #uuid "feaadcbe-e1b9-52dd-8dd7-fc16d79bdfa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34fe18e9-f3bb-5fa3-acad-95f48e7d367d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321554} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c4774f-3c94-544e-908b-0eb14e4f508a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323527} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a42e7b3-d323-5e4f-aab0-f221c70f3d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321227} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce9ae65-0f3d-54c1-be6e-2b05b2eca587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2t}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321496} {:answer/id #uuid "326eab8c-35e4-5977-b209-f7e40e3283aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321530} {:answer/id #uuid "9363d3d5-2e3e-5993-8d77-34d875a2ec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9363d3d5-2e3e-5993-8d77-34d875a2ec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322315} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66d067e-ab94-5526-aae7-9dc2d887352b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7908dcc8-2ade-5e7c-a576-33b72695a96c" :question/math-academy-id "q-365377" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2}) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2}) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-2t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-2t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt-2t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-2t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-2t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt-2t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt-2t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt-2t \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =-2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{-2t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{-2}{1-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{2}{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1356f59e-04a0-51af-b39a-7a860708c3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321298} {:answer/id #uuid "4815746e-9fe7-539d-bb32-793af7e9957f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321713} {:answer/id #uuid "66871d6a-5f30-57dc-aab8-2f465b099010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321946} {:answer/id #uuid "b6be468e-9df3-54ae-95ee-720737fa9931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322569} {:answer/id #uuid "de477895-a349-5571-bcb4-68d3a6dae781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-3}{t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6be468e-9df3-54ae-95ee-720737fa9931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322569} :answer-field/id #uuid "46bfce3a-67d1-5c28-b61a-50c95e8fe3cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323301} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "715eabdc-86cf-5d93-9a01-c244be23d0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322035} {:answer/id #uuid "8978f549-c0cc-5f49-b167-b1bbfd0d1d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322243} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f5ea56-0bf4-536f-8f77-7a8a78b9ab0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322554} {:answer/id #uuid "b651a5fc-749c-5e54-bee1-770a1f257175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322565} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9ec20f-8666-5e66-83d9-781f37295c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "715eabdc-86cf-5d93-9a01-c244be23d0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322035} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cdc0e39-ed32-5eeb-926d-95337276b33a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770dbe98-faf2-5844-9ee4-25631476a1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-365378" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ -\\frac{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0add5f-c19d-5f7d-b4b9-e7062ba28afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321236} {:answer/id #uuid "1383eca2-1d3c-5452-9f16-5b57846c8ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321302} {:answer/id #uuid "328f935e-4f79-575c-8650-32246bcbde6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(2-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321533} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdff64e-9e6d-5d36-a63a-0886d3593aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b0add5f-c19d-5f7d-b4b9-e7062ba28afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321236} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d7ecd92-2965-5e0b-8ff2-d5187ef57cc2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16113a68-63f6-51b7-80c7-694e17ec8275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321326} {:answer/id #uuid "761a80b9-a338-5077-b1a9-34500f7337d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322068} {:answer/id #uuid "c087c2ce-d887-5dc3-9fbc-556f58ff9028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322643} {:answer/id #uuid "e015adb9-200c-5008-8368-227bbc42ec0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c087c2ce-d887-5dc3-9fbc-556f58ff9028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322643} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea6c9ca6-8473-52aa-9173-527222805616" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ce629b8-462b-5d82-9660-148c84cf68f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-365383" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)(1+t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11628838-ceb0-5b74-98b6-2dd123af86b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321284} {:answer/id #uuid "964e9dbc-bbfe-59f0-8200-6778e46d6fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322344} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2a4b52-d4d4-5c3d-9b8d-ebaeed6556cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322979} {:answer/id #uuid "efdb785f-6b02-5a9c-9163-f62b35c1c8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964e9dbc-bbfe-59f0-8200-6778e46d6fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322344} :answer-field/id #uuid "01528867-afe9-5506-a703-235285b924b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08890123-cb85-52f4-b0be-9b16bcda356f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321213} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd07b27-d464-5584-b6d0-d002eb20af4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322874} {:answer/id #uuid "efdef6f2-0e4c-5436-b3aa-f2f67ad7e71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323038} {:answer/id #uuid "f762776a-c252-56bd-ae32-b06a092e03d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcd07b27-d464-5584-b6d0-d002eb20af4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322874} :answer-field/id #uuid "662ab64b-3ea7-51f2-9233-f643b9f0d225" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa5839f3-ad78-59e3-af24-cb3ab8949d40" :question/math-academy-id "q-365386" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}+3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{3} & =3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-{t}^{2}) & =(\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x{t}^{3}+1 \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-3x{t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3x{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3tx}{1-{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{3tx}{{t}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ab4742-91b1-56b7-aeb7-0cb999a0542e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321431} {:answer/id #uuid "55455322-1286-534f-9707-b6847e5a22a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321821} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea48740-8e2d-55a4-8716-3d4ab1ffab89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322144} {:answer/id #uuid "839a652d-0fac-544b-99a3-1dd16b8d6f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322185} {:answer/id #uuid "f13a54a9-d395-5742-82d1-cd829d5a84de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ea48740-8e2d-55a4-8716-3d4ab1ffab89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322144} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb28582-831f-5528-aa50-93a3ad1c3ead" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20aa476b-da98-524e-b2e5-741bcbef96ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321394} {:answer/id #uuid "21d9386f-c921-55ca-9dba-d8569e22ff55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321402} {:answer/id #uuid "220c2dd2-3764-5d1d-94c2-e69733949f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321405} {:answer/id #uuid "5b42c510-520d-5d10-b4c6-947a90b924b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321867} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d67b64-3484-53f4-bf25-b7a1f92e7c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{14t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20aa476b-da98-524e-b2e5-741bcbef96ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321394} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee8458e3-4613-5e41-8e43-3ef9d7587b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f9eb0d3-58fa-594c-8c10-ad8b6522e3fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-365388" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=3xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+7{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=3xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+7{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+7{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+7{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+3x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+14t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+14t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =(14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} \\\\ (t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =14t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba2082f-2026-51ae-946b-513ddff3f786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321483} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f1bbb-b59a-58f0-989d-7368f34c43bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322444} {:answer/id #uuid "d0aa616c-04f0-5a28-9051-a69a89515d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322777} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b49814-bd66-5fc4-af87-a060b14a242d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322832} {:answer/id #uuid "dda5ae9d-e68f-5bfa-a58b-bf49578992ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi +6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55f1bbb-b59a-58f0-989d-7368f34c43bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322444} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8778a8-2084-5de3-8964-7ea39574c75a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a7d984f-6568-5953-af07-5e17745cd0e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36559" :question/problem "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}=3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6xyz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{3}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6{x}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )(-1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{3}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )=6\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{2}(-1)^{2}(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )^{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6\\pi +(-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2})=-6{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b14a2ba-037b-5808-ab5c-1471b3b9efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322113} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6055d0-fc1a-5cd1-b93a-ac0ad546d0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322132} {:answer/id #uuid "8150aa80-6174-5846-8338-8a2330df4293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322164} {:answer/id #uuid "b39390ee-a86a-537c-be7c-ac51d056917c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322550} {:answer/id #uuid "e83c21db-4970-5c82-a74c-5bd63c4039dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b14a2ba-037b-5808-ab5c-1471b3b9efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322113} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5fd4f2-8722-5896-8d2f-2c556f5eb935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4788079-739f-5332-8879-eb48a6a8359b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36752" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{8}(7{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(7(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1423f464-beef-5e99-9409-b3d4ac5043ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321310} {:answer/id #uuid "7b755dec-3573-55e0-b187-1c70939adcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322120} {:answer/id #uuid "806e3e30-0291-58e6-abb6-ecce94953100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322156} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce25d70-41a1-50f0-91bf-535055888ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322384} {:answer/id #uuid "b718991a-198e-56d5-a300-ebd932e0ab6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b755dec-3573-55e0-b187-1c70939adcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322120} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea0db33-c7cf-5915-89f1-c5178160661f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65ec0581-72cb-57ee-83e7-13061ed24107" :question/math-academy-id "q-36947" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-9){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -27 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1369c0ac-54a3-5d47-a55e-32690a098f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321299} {:answer/id #uuid "25431ce5-f607-5144-90be-7ae6626ad54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321436} {:answer/id #uuid "42971dbc-8e46-565e-84b2-72ac7f895971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321674} {:answer/id #uuid "53cc0f39-7dba-5571-9d2a-0a77806f1ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321807} {:answer/id #uuid "85baf665-9e38-55d9-9223-b973f70f3e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25431ce5-f607-5144-90be-7ae6626ad54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7887e763-fbe8-534a-becc-d42df52a2ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82d92314-f305-5f0c-b4d3-f0a08c54fb3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37290" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)) & ={tan}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3{cot}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))}^{2}+3{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =3+3(\\frac{3}{9}) \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a29aa16-6c63-5a61-91a9-1f74b2ed3f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321225} {:answer/id #uuid "139de0b1-a7cc-5a6f-8d11-174a489bfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321305} {:answer/id #uuid "3a995098-985b-5a0d-8363-a15a9776bdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321596} {:answer/id #uuid "76e233f9-7d82-54a2-b6d4-56c363f79142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322074} {:answer/id #uuid "a544f028-4f1a-5cd3-8842-d28c764161a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a995098-985b-5a0d-8363-a15a9776bdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321596} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ac795a-9483-57f9-a1e0-931dea19d10f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f559f7f4-e02a-5090-9ef8-87c06ed535b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37399" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26853257-a15d-56bd-baf3-05cb4af2b356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} {:answer/id #uuid "6215dc3a-949b-5a88-b400-1754de3451a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321914} {:answer/id #uuid "8f37a9ee-9183-5f94-b9e6-589f18d0723c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322284} {:answer/id #uuid "97007dd8-0a0c-5e5d-b2fe-2c703399817a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322352} {:answer/id #uuid "e831241f-8157-56bb-8b58-0b3139bec9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26853257-a15d-56bd-baf3-05cb4af2b356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} :answer-field/id #uuid "001c22af-200a-5e74-a872-4fc6a4c58849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b75fe299-3a00-56ce-ac2b-3a6b8472e075" :question/math-academy-id "q-37401" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{12}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5988b748-db6c-5b36-9840-e6478cabbb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321853} {:answer/id #uuid "7c472c58-699c-5782-89ce-b06e067314de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322125} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7fab-ed01-5f57-aecb-974f3540bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} {:answer/id #uuid "d0690c0d-0bea-5716-9be4-2e7a0d6ed043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322774} {:answer/id #uuid "dc30c442-053c-588a-a4c2-b47c4d9a660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7fab-ed01-5f57-aecb-974f3540bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb178049-21d1-5f35-85d8-2469af417f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f655022-c86d-5349-9391-8c2ff380546e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37403" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{20}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2(4{x}_{3})-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d2a009-9a40-5d25-99e2-4f97af963231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321192} {:answer/id #uuid "2c533ee8-f9db-5f4a-a560-8035da471b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321490} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0552d2-cfa6-5c1e-901c-be3cad62fa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321590} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec84ef-6655-53bf-9eb0-7897a3753db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322030} {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2db87a-5385-53da-8e27-89a6fe772ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39356ac1-0a31-5e6e-9a6f-6a96bbbbe55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37683" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fa64d5-074d-5702-bcf4-064fabb99721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x-2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,x,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454955} {:answer/id #uuid "4263713e-2f96-539b-8f35-ecc62bb28c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,x,y\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455014} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5c75b7-6345-538e-96b8-2ba5d8c8cf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455041} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac4954c-967a-55e2-9e2d-f8a985c1ff0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455063} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c02bca-de60-5fa3-8668-018b71b4b4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,y,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e5c75b7-6345-538e-96b8-2ba5d8c8cf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455041} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7e412b-267f-58a3-94eb-d3421d219bcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d0e70ad-1c81-5568-a326-e79afb6bb189" :question/math-academy-id "q-37827" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b726bfa-ba4c-5d91-9f6d-6a3764d8755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321242} {:answer/id #uuid "1594e17d-7011-5f7d-aa6a-12336ec25a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321322} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} {:answer/id #uuid "564621d9-f224-5fff-b159-cf3615e51a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321828} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f698ad-bda0-5b89-8175-c64721d84bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb45d35-7041-5bf4-8524-44adec58ddbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad4a8e76-c297-5cff-8131-747df131b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-37865" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4cafde-db4c-54bb-bab3-013f762c6d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321731} {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf875a9-930b-5577-b821-7ead99c40d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321884} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe73b65-ebc3-5cff-9a52-a69da7741fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322150} {:answer/id #uuid "b124652f-7ab9-5b4c-9413-d3dfdcb4c6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce29bc07-9e48-58fd-9669-3eeb517f79e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "565688d3-a42b-5b17-9265-a372de4b8754" :question/math-academy-id "q-37899" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe point $P$ lies on the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{3}{7},$ use the Pythagorean Identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{3}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264c2a12-e0c5-5a72-8a56-3afd346a3797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321444} {:answer/id #uuid "27c94ab9-3f27-5b94-9153-03a51a8506f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321458} {:answer/id #uuid "28553fdc-9d2d-5736-815e-a08f21b3eb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321462} {:answer/id #uuid "892d722c-b7e1-5e03-b74a-cd70d864499f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322240} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bbf3fb-8f2a-5c40-ba48-859a8ad13ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "264c2a12-e0c5-5a72-8a56-3afd346a3797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321444} :answer-field/id #uuid "6846a47f-81a7-5c49-bfa1-5b3dcabbb036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a9ac202-6009-5fae-8bac-c5238fbadec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37947" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4,$ then what is $f(\\frac{1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{1}{2})={lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2328240e-4257-5bd9-a135-e92fd6cfcac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321415} {:answer/id #uuid "4590da6a-1d19-5e9e-8708-8e979eef5674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321698} {:answer/id #uuid "76e89d5a-25ad-5e91-8f79-538c049f2947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322075} {:answer/id #uuid "8602bb92-aa80-513a-bc71-45eb070984a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322211} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c7eeb-1587-561c-88b4-d2fdd44746eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c7eeb-1587-561c-88b4-d2fdd44746eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322870} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc079157-7349-5c1d-b71e-8d2f3ca017ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "756c4008-7e12-5f70-b039-f70f52bc5864" :question/math-academy-id "q-37951" :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral\n${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{\\pi }{3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{\\pi }{3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[-\\frac{\\pi }{3y}]}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot (-1)}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot (-2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640f91af-9af3-58fc-8a63-9b8699619aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324801} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b6266a-0178-58c9-afc0-b11ab26e4082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325351} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbf60c-21a8-5de6-bc41-6cabb8ede0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325839} {:answer/id #uuid "ede03381-3d73-5674-a3cc-1b91d88fe642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326063} {:answer/id #uuid "ef576b5f-b070-572f-99c9-6414e6f1049a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbf60c-21a8-5de6-bc41-6cabb8ede0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325839} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ee238c-35a5-5dad-bb56-ba165c7577fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12b9ca6b-0ddb-5ee4-aeb2-e1fcfdc8bcb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3850" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x{e}^{-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c8a1483-7aea-506a-a4fc-1ccec710489d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324212} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4ea4bf-f87b-5b48-92dc-27fd4fef8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324350} {:answer/id #uuid "30f7adc2-38f4-5f80-b3df-1c30ce0e7a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324362} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe7a3e7-23fc-5974-bc1a-f0da89ca6543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324626} {:answer/id #uuid "5e65b2e2-013e-5ebf-89da-b39afccbf471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4ea4bf-f87b-5b48-92dc-27fd4fef8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324350} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ec30624-d599-5c74-9abb-9ad63322e7da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "592b6ac1-ddb2-56aa-ae83-1783ef1ea8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-38629" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $w-x+y,$ where $w,x,y,z$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}w-3x-2z \\\\ 2x-2w-4y \\\\ 3w-y-10x-7z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-8.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cc5ee2-6da9-52c1-a92b-8edb26634139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} {:answer/id #uuid "47e93b3f-33d7-5090-b1fc-02844e927bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324553} {:answer/id #uuid "a44579f4-a9e4-53da-a506-9eefbb4bf2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325367} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8f6f17-d42e-553d-a74b-0e76268b5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325601} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b95922-aad1-5b77-a0d0-e6a698ab713b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cc5ee2-6da9-52c1-a92b-8edb26634139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} :answer-field/id #uuid "36035083-9336-58a8-9968-10256743cb12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "688a2834-9fcd-5275-97a0-97e7aaa1b79c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38704" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+(-\\frac{1}{6})=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4752b046-3b7e-5b8b-bc64-961b93ceeafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb82d2af5bba5f24727e80c1e748c9a830d9ea89dde2f641f7cc3062006d94a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324549} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd16a56-1a91-577c-937f-a6234f7de2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c2409230292f147b3f0f87dfda1fbbf48ad3d7f718dbe315b55c5fde04d6bf4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325330} {:answer/id #uuid "c3efa590-2060-5310-8fcd-6ed4b394e490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/38be17e7358b863fd00aa77b27a5d13d35e95f9553337fc71b21b1b65c2e8e6c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325655} {:answer/id #uuid "d31e12d5-0281-5042-a3db-54c4f4cf1403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f12ef2075acd21307696bc9c7920ab72730662d4b3d7ff090df52f81c33ffa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325805} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ab9bda-564c-5a98-9a03-afbb6e1d45a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40f4e01f3e6f6f52d781e784c8788c6688fe0ea02d5164d3d19f46b2758fc895.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4752b046-3b7e-5b8b-bc64-961b93ceeafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb82d2af5bba5f24727e80c1e748c9a830d9ea89dde2f641f7cc3062006d94a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324549} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3f99be-3ea4-5937-ab62-cf6cec0982a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7da895d-6757-5ad2-9849-d7013a6b1846" :question/math-academy-id "q-39247" :question/problem "![](images/88/88be779d85ba51746abba7a71cad58b6986fd72a14ba4fc09e8b015494f57c0e.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix. So, we need to find its scale factor and the angle of rotation.\n\nWe can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ clockwise by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$  and scale the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f7e7fa8d149ae9b6f07af57cbc8fc103b2d7c306aec98dbb2119145aa276ca.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9c9635-514f-5437-89a3-2311516df929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324616} {:answer/id #uuid "542f73e6-a0ee-5f63-a36b-e67fde3a6341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324669} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa4c2bd-e1e8-5ef1-b567-38d573db5658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324998} {:answer/id #uuid "98e23fb5-b473-5394-a5ba-4656c5c07d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325270} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e0be4-5f46-5ddf-98a2-9f9d363b7d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e0be4-5f46-5ddf-98a2-9f9d363b7d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325323} :answer-field/id #uuid "526c7e14-1815-5fc1-a1ad-f65ad4c3be74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d6ca191-15db-5023-b8de-7e13937e634b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39422" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,dt \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-2(\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f28ae9-ad9a-5c26-9668-970408c70e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $29$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324412} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5c3799-5e4f-5a34-b947-5cfae02078cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $30$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324622} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bed55f-f824-5e81-9b0b-d1dabfeb7799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $31$st term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325524} {:answer/id #uuid "ca97757a-df6b-59a0-b351-58dd173e396f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $28$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325727} {:answer/id #uuid "f1773241-3243-58c4-b192-f56ea9ba3f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $33$rd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f5c3799-5e4f-5a34-b947-5cfae02078cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $30$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324622} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b37db49-95d4-5e02-bb33-93b46e7afabc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "149ce21a-affa-5de1-9e5b-30002aa3b866" :question/math-academy-id "q-39647" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,2$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n18,\\,12,\\,8,\\,\\frac{16}{3},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=18$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{12}{18}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,2.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ 18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,2}{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1≈29.159\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $30$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,2.$"}]
